@namespace FamilyHQ.WebUi.Components.Dashboard

@* One event whose reminders were set on the event itself, read as "[when] · title · [who]". Nothing
   on the row describes the reminders: the FILTER behind this list is what makes it a reminders view,
   and the family asked for the bell glyph and the reminder-summary line to go.

   data-event-id lets the E2E suite address a row by what it IS rather than by its position in the
   list — rows reorder as events start and finish, so a positional selector would silently drift to
   the wrong row. It alone is enough to identify a row: there is exactly one row per event. *@
<div class="reminder-row"
     data-testid="reminder-row"
     data-event-id="@Event.EventId"
     @onclick="() => OnOpenDay.InvokeAsync(Event.EventDate.ToDateTime(TimeOnly.MinValue))">
    <span class="reminder-row__time">@ReminderRowDisplay.EventTime(Event.EventDate, Event.EventTimeOfDay, Today)</span>
    <span class="reminder-row__title">@Event.EventTitle</span>
    @* The server substitutes the owning calendar's name as a member when it can resolve one, so an
       empty Members list means it could not — there is no calendar name anywhere to fall back to,
       not just none wired to this view. Rendering nothing here is the honest answer; inventing a
       placeholder ("this calendar") would assert something meaningless to a family reading a wall
       display, which is worse than admitting the gap — the same reasoning behind both of the
       standing footnotes below. *@
    <div class="reminder-row__people">
        @foreach (var member in Event.Members)
        {
            <span @key="member.DisplayName"
                  class="reminder-member-chip"
                  style="--chip-color: @(member.Color ?? "var(--theme-accent)")">@member.DisplayName</span>
        }
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The event this row renders. Required — there is no meaningful empty row.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public UpcomingReminderEventViewModel Event { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>
    /// The date the row's label is measured against, which is all the row needs to lead with
    /// "Today", "Tomorrow" or its own day and date (<see cref="ReminderRowDisplay.EventTime"/>). The
    /// row is never told which panel it was rendered into, and does not need to be: the label is a
    /// fact about the event's own date measured against this one, so a row filed under "Next month"
    /// on the last day of a month still reads "Tomorrow" — which is exactly when the family must not
    /// lose it.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public DateOnly Today { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised with the event's own day when the row is tapped, so the dashboard drills into the Day
    /// view exactly as the Month and Agenda views' rows do. The whole row is the tap target.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Typed <see cref="DateTime"/> because the Month, Day and Agenda views' drill-downs are, and the
    /// dashboard's selected date is. A row only knows a date, so midnight is the only honest time to
    /// pair with it — and the destination reads the date alone.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<DateTime> OnOpenDay { get; set; }
}
